%description:

RFC 9293 — a connection opens with the three-way handshake.

Checks RFC9293-EST-1, RFC9293-EST-2, RFC9293-SEQ-1, RFC9293-HDR-1 (description) and
RFC9293-CKSUM-1 (must), and covers RFC9293-ISS-1 (description) and RFC9293-OPT-1 (should)
from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9293/catalog.md. The English procedure and the mockup
are in doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks.md#connection-establishment; the program
steps below map one to one onto its expected observations. Mapping of the mockup: host A =
host1, host B = host2, link L = host1-host2.

The governing document is RFC 9293 (August 2022), which obsoletes RFC 793. See
doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/standards.md.

No step asserts an absolute sequence number. Each side picks its own initial sequence
number (RFC9293-ISS-1), so every expectation is a captured value plus an offset.

Deviation on observation 1: the header-length and checksum conditions (RFC9293-HDR-1,
RFC9293-CKSUM-1) cannot be observed at host1.tcp's own outbound signal, so this observation
moves to host1.eth[0].mac. TCP's checksum, when checksumMode = "computed", is inserted by
TcpChecksumInsertionHook::datagramPostRoutingHook() (src/inet/transportlayer/tcp_common/
TcpChecksumInsertionHook.cc), a hook that runs during the IPv4 module's own postRouting
stage -- strictly after host1.tcp has already emitted packetSentToLowerSignal and hosted the
segment to IP. Observed at host1.tcp the checksum field would therefore still read its
unset default of 0. The header length is unaffected by this (it is fixed when the header is
built), but both conditions belong to the one expected observation, so both move together.
Observation 3 needs only the header length, which has no such timing dependency, so it stays
at host1.tcp. The captured header length is reported in the run: 24 octets means host A's
SYN carries the MSS option (RFC9293-OPT-1, a "should"); this test does not assert either
value, since the option's absence would be a note, not a failure.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "PacketField.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc9293_connection_establishment")
        // Observation 1: host A sends the SYN. Record ISS_A and the header length. This
        // also confirms the stimulus -- without the SYN the rest of the check would pass
        // vacuously. A header of at least 20 octets and a nonzero checksum cover HDR-1 and
        // CKSUM-1; observed at the MAC because the checksum isn't inserted until the IPv4
        // module's postRouting stage (see the %description deviation note).
        .once(on("host1.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false && tcp.headerLength >= 20B && tcp.checksum != 0")
                  .capture("issA", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  // Captured via a lambda, stripping the "B" unit into a plain number: the
                  // declarative string-form capture("name", "tcp.headerLength") stores the
                  // field's unit-bearing cValue as-is, and EventPattern.cc's
                  // formatCaptureValue() -- used to substitute {captures} into a *later*
                  // step's dynamic expression -- calls cValue::intValue(), which throws
                  // ("Attempt to use the value '24B' as a dimensionless number", confirmed
                  // empirically) for any capture that still carries a unit, even one the
                  // later step's own expression never references. No prior test in this
                  // suite captured a unit-bearing field, so this is new territory.
                  .capture("synHeaderLength", [](const PacketEvent& e) {
                      return cValue(evalPacketField(e.packet, "tcp.headerLength").doubleValueInUnit("B"));
                  })
                  .describe("host A's SYN, carrying ISS_A, a header of at least 20 octets, and a nonzero checksum").within(0.5))
        // Observation 2: the SYN+ACK acknowledges ISS_A + 1. The plus one is
        // RFC9293-SEQ-1: the SYN occupied exactly one sequence number.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .packet("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.ackNo == {issA} + 1")
                  .capture("issB", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .describe("host B's SYN+ACK acknowledging ISS_A + 1").within(0.5))
        // Observation 3: host A's final ACK acknowledges ISS_B + 1, with a 20-octet header:
        // no option, five words.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.synBit == false && tcp.ackNo == {issB} + 1 && tcp.headerLength == 20B")
                  .describe("host A's final ACK acknowledging ISS_B + 1, with a 20-octet header").within(0.5));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9293_connection_establishment compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The mockup of doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks.md#common-mockup:
// host A -- link L -- host B.
network Rfc9293TcpNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc9293TcpNet
sim-time-limit = 2s
seed-set = 0

# Static ARP keeps the wire free of ARP request/reply noise (also needed since observation 1
# now reads the checksum at the MAC layer).
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# Every node computes real TCP checksums (checks.md's common mockup: the checksum is never
# optional, MUST-2). The default "declared" mode never inserts a hook at all (see
# src/inet/transportlayer/tcp/Tcp.cc), so the checksum field would stay at its unset 0.
**.checksumMode = "computed"

# The scenario constants of checks.md#common-mockup.
*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 100B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = 0.4s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpEchoApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9293_connection_establishment: PASS
